  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality ${(0.84)}^{x+5}-20>0.$",
  "worked_solution": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(0.84)}^{x+5}-20 & >0 \\\\ {(0.84)}^{x+5} & >20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061[(0.84)^{x+5}] & >\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(20) \\\\ (x+5)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.84) & >\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.84)$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & <\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(20)}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.84)} \\\\ x & <\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(20)}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.84)}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$",
